1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,450
Have you ever wondered why, when you
get sick, you can simply go to a doctor?

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,790
You might have to pay.
You might have to wait.

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:09,950
You might have to deal with paperwork.

4
00:00:10,962 --> 00:00:13,042
But the idea that treatment
is something you can

5
00:00:13,122 --> 00:00:16,012
claim when you need it
seems completely normal.

6
00:00:16,733 --> 00:00:20,293
It isn't. In historical
terms, public health insurance

7
00:00:20,373 --> 00:00:22,903
is a surprisingly recent invention.

8
00:00:23,828 --> 00:00:26,468
To understand why, we
need to go back to a time

9
00:00:26,548 --> 00:00:29,058
when getting sick meant
something very different.

10
00:00:29,876 --> 00:00:33,206
Imagine living in Europe
around eighteen hundred.

11
00:00:33,576 --> 00:00:36,236
You are a craftsman, a farm worker, or one

12
00:00:36,316 --> 00:00:38,326
of the growing number
working in a factory.

13
00:00:39,211 --> 00:00:41,721
You get seriously ill. You can't work.

14
00:00:41,811 --> 00:00:44,541
And because you can't
work, you don't get paid.

15
00:00:45,368 --> 00:00:47,238
There is no national health service.

16
00:00:47,508 --> 00:00:49,018
No insurance card.

17
00:00:49,548 --> 00:00:51,318
There may not even be a doctor nearby.

18
00:00:52,271 --> 00:00:54,461
If you have money, you
can pay for treatment.

19
00:00:55,351 --> 00:00:57,321
If you are poor, you depend on charity.

20
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:01,550
And if the illness lasts for months,
you have a much bigger problem.

21
00:01:02,120 --> 00:01:05,290
You are not just sick.
You have lost your income.

22
00:01:08,090 --> 00:01:11,220
The basic idea of sharing
risk is actually ancient.

23
00:01:12,197 --> 00:01:14,947
In the Roman world there
were mutual associations.

24
00:01:15,937 --> 00:01:19,487
Members paid in every month and could
get help when something went wrong.

25
00:01:20,297 --> 00:01:22,087
Some of them paid for funerals.

26
00:01:22,814 --> 00:01:25,044
Medieval guilds continued this.

27
00:01:25,854 --> 00:01:29,524
A guild controlled a trade, but it
was also a primitive safety net.

28
00:01:30,174 --> 00:01:34,964
A member who became ill might receive
assistance. So might his widow.

29
00:01:35,801 --> 00:01:38,251
So the basic principle was already there.

30
00:01:39,361 --> 00:01:40,751
Everyone contributes a little.

31
00:01:41,561 --> 00:01:45,391
And when one member suffers a
misfortune, the common fund helps them.

32
00:01:46,077 --> 00:01:48,107
But this wasn't modern health insurance.

33
00:01:48,957 --> 00:01:50,427
The communities were small.

34
00:01:50,577 --> 00:01:53,947
Membership was voluntary.
And the help was limited.

35
00:01:56,667 --> 00:01:59,477
Then the Industrial
Revolution changed everything.

36
00:02:00,307 --> 00:02:03,157
It didn't just create
factories and steam engines.

37
00:02:03,467 --> 00:02:06,817
It changed the relationship
between people and risk.

38
00:02:07,605 --> 00:02:09,635
Millions of people moved to cities.

39
00:02:09,985 --> 00:02:13,735
They no longer lived in villages
where the whole family lived nearby.

40
00:02:14,679 --> 00:02:16,329
They became wage workers.

41
00:02:16,439 --> 00:02:20,409
And for a wage worker, illness
could be financially devastating.

42
00:02:21,539 --> 00:02:24,569
So workers began creating
their own organisations.

43
00:02:25,099 --> 00:02:29,429
Mutual aid societies. Friendly
societies. Sickness funds.

44
00:02:30,339 --> 00:02:32,689
They contributed a small amount regularly.

45
00:02:33,759 --> 00:02:38,209
And if one of them became ill, the fund
could provide money, or pay for treatment.

46
00:02:39,013 --> 00:02:41,603
This was much closer to
modern health insurance.

47
00:02:41,953 --> 00:02:45,623
But it was still voluntary, and
it still didn't cover everyone.

48
00:02:48,553 --> 00:02:52,643
By the late nineteenth century,
Germany was a great industrial power.

49
00:02:53,393 --> 00:02:55,883
And its government was
worried about something else.

50
00:02:56,813 --> 00:02:59,063
The growing socialist
and workers' movement.

51
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:01,967
Enter Otto von Bismarck.

52
00:03:02,277 --> 00:03:03,557
Bismarck was not a socialist.

53
00:03:03,637 --> 00:03:05,027
Quite the opposite.

54
00:03:05,967 --> 00:03:11,237
In eighteen seventy-eight, his government
banned socialist organisations outright.

55
00:03:12,229 --> 00:03:16,289
He was a conservative who wanted to
preserve the existing political order.

56
00:03:16,369 --> 00:03:19,279
And he realised something rather clever.

57
00:03:20,049 --> 00:03:22,629
If workers had protection
against the risks of industrial

58
00:03:22,709 --> 00:03:25,999
life, they might have less
reason for revolution.

59
00:03:26,929 --> 00:03:31,109
So Germany introduced compulsory sickness
insurance in eighteen eighty-three.

60
00:03:31,189 --> 00:03:33,639
Five years after the ban.

61
00:03:34,409 --> 00:03:35,949
It is generally considered one of the

62
00:03:36,029 --> 00:03:38,719
foundations of modern
public health insurance.

63
00:03:39,309 --> 00:03:41,879
Workers and employers paid in.

64
00:03:42,269 --> 00:03:45,279
The funds paid for treatment
and sickness benefits.

65
00:03:46,157 --> 00:03:48,787
It was not free healthcare.
It was insurance.

66
00:03:49,117 --> 00:03:51,307
And that distinction is important.

67
00:03:52,399 --> 00:03:54,029
The idea was not this.

68
00:03:54,599 --> 00:03:57,109
The government will simply
pay for everyone's healthcare.

69
00:03:58,385 --> 00:03:59,585
It was this.

70
00:03:59,685 --> 00:04:04,435
Everyone in the covered group pays in, and
the fund pays when someone becomes ill.

71
00:04:05,160 --> 00:04:07,450
This became known as the Bismarck model.

72
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:13,330
The German experiment
was hugely influential.

73
00:04:15,025 --> 00:04:18,115
Austria-Hungary introduced sickness
insurance a few years later.

74
00:04:18,845 --> 00:04:21,875
Britain introduced the National
Insurance system in nineteen eleven.

75
00:04:23,343 --> 00:04:26,623
And after the First World
War, Czechoslovakia inherited

76
00:04:26,703 --> 00:04:29,993
a system based on the earlier
Austro-Hungarian model.

77
00:04:30,757 --> 00:04:32,747
The general idea was spreading.

78
00:04:33,457 --> 00:04:35,847
Healthcare could be treated
as a collective risk.

79
00:04:38,517 --> 00:04:41,607
There is another famous model,
and it came from Britain.

80
00:04:42,559 --> 00:04:45,219
During the Second World
War, the economist William

81
00:04:45,299 --> 00:04:47,929
Beveridge proposed a
very different approach.

82
00:04:48,778 --> 00:04:51,368
Instead of insurance
contributions, healthcare

83
00:04:51,618 --> 00:04:53,748
could be funded through general taxation.

84
00:04:54,805 --> 00:04:58,115
The result was the National Health
Service, created in nineteen forty-eight.

85
00:04:59,978 --> 00:05:03,728
And now there were two major
approaches. The Bismarck model.

86
00:05:04,478 --> 00:05:06,408
You pay into health insurance funds.

87
00:05:07,329 --> 00:05:11,779
The Beveridge model. You pay taxes,
and the government finances healthcare.

88
00:05:13,230 --> 00:05:15,260
Neither system appeared naturally.

89
00:05:16,030 --> 00:05:17,580
Both were political choices.

90
00:05:18,597 --> 00:05:21,127
And both were responses
to the same problem.

91
00:05:21,837 --> 00:05:23,447
Modern medicine is expensive.

92
00:05:23,917 --> 00:05:25,347
Illness is unpredictable.

93
00:05:26,277 --> 00:05:29,967
And almost nobody can save enough
for every possible medical disaster.

94
00:05:33,347 --> 00:05:37,037
So what happened in the
United States? Well...

95
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:40,130
The United States did
not choose either model.

96
00:05:40,900 --> 00:05:43,430
It gradually built something
much more complicated.

97
00:05:44,445 --> 00:05:48,075
Today, Americans get healthcare
through several different systems.

98
00:05:48,745 --> 00:05:50,095
There is private insurance.

99
00:05:50,985 --> 00:05:53,115
There is employer-sponsored insurance.

100
00:05:54,017 --> 00:05:56,827
There is Medicare, mainly
for older Americans.

101
00:05:57,617 --> 00:06:02,727
There is Medicaid for eligible low-income
people, with the rules varying by state.

102
00:06:03,544 --> 00:06:05,794
There is the Veterans
Health Administration.

103
00:06:05,944 --> 00:06:09,614
And millions of people still
pay large amounts themselves.

104
00:06:10,682 --> 00:06:12,352
So when someone says this.

105
00:06:12,482 --> 00:06:14,622
America has private healthcare.

106
00:06:14,702 --> 00:06:16,292
That's not really accurate.

107
00:06:17,159 --> 00:06:18,819
And when someone says this.

108
00:06:18,899 --> 00:06:21,039
America has public healthcare.

109
00:06:21,119 --> 00:06:22,829
That's not accurate either.

110
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:25,000
America has both.

111
00:06:25,710 --> 00:06:28,620
The United States never completely
rejected public healthcare.

112
00:06:30,048 --> 00:06:33,148
It built public programmes for
particular groups, while much

113
00:06:33,228 --> 00:06:36,298
of the rest of the population
relied on private insurance.

114
00:06:37,488 --> 00:06:39,518
A giant historical patchwork.

115
00:06:42,258 --> 00:06:45,428
And one part of that patchwork
is especially strange.

116
00:06:46,238 --> 00:06:50,308
In much of Europe, changing your job
doesn't mean losing your health insurance.

117
00:06:51,252 --> 00:06:54,432
In the United States,
employer-sponsored health insurance

118
00:06:54,512 --> 00:06:57,722
became the dominant way
many Americans got coverage.

119
00:06:58,537 --> 00:07:00,527
And it happened almost by accident.

120
00:07:01,477 --> 00:07:04,507
During the Second World War,
the government froze wages.

121
00:07:05,462 --> 00:07:09,432
Factories were desperate for workers
but were not allowed to pay them more.

122
00:07:10,062 --> 00:07:12,512
So they offered health insurance instead.

123
00:07:13,389 --> 00:07:14,819
It didn't count as wages.

124
00:07:15,189 --> 00:07:18,159
And it wasn't taxed like wages either.

125
00:07:18,609 --> 00:07:22,079
By the end of the war, the number
of Americans covered had tripled.

126
00:07:22,919 --> 00:07:26,109
Nobody had chosen this as
a national health policy.

127
00:07:26,919 --> 00:07:28,309
So you change jobs...

128
00:07:28,399 --> 00:07:32,349
and your healthcare changes
with it. Lose your job...

129
00:07:32,599 --> 00:07:35,309
and you may have to find
another way to get insured.

130
00:07:36,177 --> 00:07:39,687
It is the result of historical
decisions piling up over decades.

131
00:07:42,867 --> 00:07:44,317
Imagine you are sick.

132
00:07:45,067 --> 00:07:48,737
In a typical European system, the
basic question is usually this.

133
00:07:49,567 --> 00:07:50,917
Where can I get treatment?

134
00:07:51,821 --> 00:07:54,211
In the United States,
there are several more.

135
00:07:54,981 --> 00:07:56,491
Is the doctor in my network?

136
00:07:57,141 --> 00:07:59,011
Does my insurance cover this?

137
00:07:59,771 --> 00:08:01,241
Have I reached my deductible?

138
00:08:02,131 --> 00:08:03,881
How much will the hospital charge?

139
00:08:04,731 --> 00:08:06,561
And how much will I actually pay?

140
00:08:07,359 --> 00:08:09,799
The result is that two people
getting the same treatment

141
00:08:09,879 --> 00:08:13,229
can have completely different
financial experiences.

142
00:08:16,429 --> 00:08:19,259
So why did Europe go one
way and America another?

143
00:08:19,589 --> 00:08:24,519
There isn't one simple answer.
History matters. Institutions matter.

144
00:08:25,423 --> 00:08:27,353
And timing matters most of all.

145
00:08:28,163 --> 00:08:30,723
Once a country builds a
healthcare system, millions

146
00:08:30,803 --> 00:08:34,373
of people, hospitals and
insurers adapt around it.

147
00:08:35,003 --> 00:08:37,753
Changing it later becomes
enormously difficult.

148
00:08:38,665 --> 00:08:40,915
Nobody invented the correct system.

149
00:08:41,565 --> 00:08:43,695
They simply started from different places.

150
00:08:46,835 --> 00:08:48,765
And this brings us back to the beginning.

151
00:08:49,675 --> 00:08:54,425
Health insurance feels like something
that must have always existed. It hasn't.

152
00:08:55,082 --> 00:08:59,262
For most of human history, there was no
health insurance. There were families.

153
00:08:59,342 --> 00:09:03,132
Communities. Charities.
Guilds. Mutual aid societies.

154
00:09:04,223 --> 00:09:06,813
And people simply paid
doctors when they could.

155
00:09:07,723 --> 00:09:10,083
Modern public health
insurance is largely a

156
00:09:10,163 --> 00:09:12,513
product of the last
hundred and fifty years.

157
00:09:13,324 --> 00:09:17,114
It emerged because industrial
society created a new problem.

158
00:09:17,864 --> 00:09:20,824
A person could be perfectly
able to work, and one

159
00:09:20,904 --> 00:09:23,714
illness could still destroy
their family's finances.

160
00:09:24,771 --> 00:09:26,291
Insurance provided a solution.

161
00:09:26,371 --> 00:09:28,161
Instead of asking this.

162
00:09:28,451 --> 00:09:31,021
Can this person afford to be sick?

163
00:09:31,824 --> 00:09:33,494
Society could ask this.

164
00:09:34,144 --> 00:09:36,594
Can all of us share the
risk of becoming sick?

165
00:09:37,426 --> 00:09:41,446
And that is what public health
insurance is. Not free healthcare.

166
00:09:41,626 --> 00:09:43,316
Not government-owned hospitals.

167
00:09:44,306 --> 00:09:46,266
But a mechanism for
turning an unpredictable

168
00:09:46,346 --> 00:09:50,556
individual disaster into a
collective financial risk.

169
00:09:51,250 --> 00:09:53,910
And once you look at it
that way, the strange thing

170
00:09:53,990 --> 00:09:56,620
isn't that different countries
have different systems.

171
00:09:57,533 --> 00:10:01,363
The strange thing is that we ever
managed to create systems at all.

172
00:10:02,045 --> 00:10:04,255
Because for almost all of human history...

173
00:10:04,965 --> 00:10:07,375
if you got sick, you were
basically on your own.
